<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02P9">Definition <span data-tag="02P9">9.1.0.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category. We say that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>filtered</em> if it satisfies the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(\ast _0)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is nonempty. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(\ast _1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, there exists an object $Z \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and a pair of morphisms $u: X \rightarrow Z$ and $v: Y \rightarrow Z$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(\ast _2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every pair of morphisms $f_0, f_1: X \rightarrow Y$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ having the same source and target, there exists a morphism $g: Y \rightarrow Z$ satisfying $g \circ f_0 = g \circ f_1$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
